From 1fcd5d8629c2df145f9c5f4a391ce3959cea5149 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: WordCraft CN <dev@local>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 19:05:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix(docx): keep a run's East Asian language
 (w:lang/@w:eastAsia)

Word writes the three language attributes of a run together: w:val for
Latin text, w:eastAsia for Chinese, Japanese and Korean text, and w:bidi
for complex script. The model had a slot for the first and the last
only, so w:eastAsia was read and then dropped, and a document Word saved
lost its Chinese language on the way through.

That matters outside WordCraft too: with w:eastAsia gone, Word proofs a
Chinese paragraph with the Latin rules, picks a Latin font for it, and
ignores the East Asian typography options.

Add CharProps.lang_ea next to lang_bidi, read w:lang/@w:eastAsia, write
all three attributes back, and cover it with a round-trip test.

Verified: a run carrying w:val="en-US" w:eastAsia="zh-CN" w:bidi="ar-SA"
now survives wordcraft-cli convert unchanged; before, w:eastAsia was lost.
